on of Bikes',url:'https://teob.dk'}})} /></head><body><EnglishHeader current="stories" danishPath="/historier/da-tour-de-france-flyttede-ind-paa-boernevaerelset/"/><main><article class="mini-page">
<header class="mini-hero"><p class="section-label">Toys · Tour de France · from the 1930s</p><h1>When the Tour de France moved into the <em>children’s room</em></h1><p>A few centimetres of metal, two thin wheels and a flat base were enough to stage an entire bicycle race.</p></header>
<figure class="mini-lead"><OptimizedImage src={hero} alt="Collection of miniature cyclists displayed in a cabinet" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" widths={[480,768,1024,1440,1824]}/><figcaption>The collection ranges from older cast riders to later models wearing familiar team jerseys. Photo: The Evolution of Bikes.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="mini-opening"><p class="section-label">A road drawn in chalk</p><div><p>The course might run along a pavement, through a sandpit or across a carpet. Chalk made the bends. A pile of sand became a mountain pass. The finish required little more than a line and somebody keeping track of the order.</p><p>Small cyclists stood on the course. Some were flicked directly with a finger. Others followed a marble shot along the route. Children in France, Belgium, the Netherlands and Italy made their own stage races and kept careful records of winners, times and jerseys. The Tour could continue after the radio had been switched off and the real peloton had moved on.</p></div></section>
<section class="mini-split"><div><p class="section-label">From soldier to cyclist</p><h2>The foundries found a new hero</h2><figure class="mini-side-figure"><OptimizedImage src={frenchRear} alt="Older hand-painted miniature cyclist in French colours seen from behind" widths={[280,420,560]}/><figcaption>A hand-painted rider in French colours.</figcaption></figure></div><div><p>Cast figures were already familiar toys in the 1930s and 1940s. Soldiers, firemen and animals left small workshops in large numbers. Popular enthusiasm for the Tour de France, the Giro d’Italia and road racing gave manufacturers an obvious new subject.</p><p>The earliest riders were often described as lead figures. As restrictions on lead in toys became stricter, other materials took over. The French Fonderie Roger made miniature cyclists in zamak during the 1950s. Zamak is a zinc alloy containing aluminium, magnesium and copper, well suited to die casting. Plastic cyclists later appeared from Roger, Cofalu, Salza and others.</p><p>This was a small object but not necessarily a small business. Fonderie Roger states that by the mid-1960s it was producing several hundred thousand cyclists a year for France, Italy and Belgium.</p></div></section>
<section class="mini-gallery mini-gallery--three"><figure><OptimizedImage src={earlyGreen} alt="Worn hand-painted miniature cyclist on a blue bicycle" widths={[320,480,700]}/><figcaption>Wear in paint and metal forms part of the history of many early figures.</figcaption></figure><figure><OptimizedImage src={earlyFrance} alt="Miniature cyclist in French colours riding in a low position" widths={[320,480,700]}/><figcaption>Team and national colours made riders recognisable in the homemade peloton.</figcaption></figure><figure><OptimizedImage src={longNose} alt="Older miniature cyclist with a notably long nose" widths={[320,480,700]}/><figcaption>Collectors associate the pronounced nose with the term “Long Nose”.</figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="mini-band"><div><strong>5 cm</strong><span>could hold an entire Tour hero</span></div><p>The figure had to stand, move and survive play. Its base mattered as much as the fine paintwork.</p></section>
<section class="mini-split mini-split--reverse"><div><p class="section-label">The game</p><h2>The stage race made its own rules</h2><figure class="mini-side-figure"><OptimizedImage src={yellowRouleur} alt="Miniature cyclist in a yellow jersey on a cast base" widths={[280,420,560]}/><figcaption>A rouleur on the stable elongated base.</figcaption></figure></div><div><p>There was no single set of rules. Some children used dice. Others flicked the rider or let a marble decide how far it could advance. A bump on the beach became a climb, while cracks in the pavement supplied all the cobbles a race could need.</p><p>The important point was that the game could grow. It might be one classic or an entire Tour, complete with stages, mountains classification, green jersey and overall time. Results went into notebooks. These figures were not merely objects to look at. They were pieces in a game devised and managed by the children themselves.</p><p>Some models had a central square base and could serve as board-game pieces. The better-known triangular base offered stability outdoors and was eventually cast as part of the rider so that it could not be lost along the route.</p></div></section>
<figure class="mini-wide"><OptimizedImage src={wornRider} alt="Heavily worn older miniature cyclist in a blue, white and red jersey" widths={[480,768,1024,1440]}/><figcaption>A used rider may have lost much of its paint and still be ready on its cast base. Photo: The Evolution of Bikes.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="mini-split"><div><p class="section-label">The positions</p><h2>A whole peloton from a few moulds</h2><figure class="mini-side-figure"><OptimizedImage src={bianchiRider} alt="Later miniature cyclist in a Bianchi jersey" widths={[280,420,560]}/><figcaption>A later model with team name and more painted detail.</figcaption></figure></div><div><p>Manufacturers developed a number of basic poses. The <em>rouleur</em> was seated in a normal riding position. A <em>grimpeur</em> or <em>danseur</em> stood on the pedals. The sprinter crouched low over the bars, while the <em>vainqueur</em> raised both arms in victory. A <em>bidon</em> figure reached for or drank from a bottle.</p><p>Colour did the rest. The same casting could become a French champion, a yellow jersey or a familiar sponsored team. Figures were often hand-painted, and small differences in faces, jerseys and brushwork gave each one its own character.</p><p>Collectors’ terms also preserve traces of production. “LN”, or “Long Nose”, is used for figures with an unusually long or bulbous nose. It is commonly attributed to a worn mould. What began as an irregular detail later became something to seek out.</p></div></section>
<figure class="mini-box"><OptimizedImage src={boxedWinner} alt="Miniature cyclist raising both arms in its original Champion box" widths={[400,620,860]}/><figcaption>A later <em>vainqueur</em> at 1:43 scale: victory became a standard pose too.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="mini-split mini-split--reverse"><div><p class="section-label">The caravan in miniature</p><h2>The cars joined the race</h2><figure class="mini-side-figure mini-side-figure--wide"><OptimizedImage src={mediaCars} alt="Three miniature press and camera cars from bicycle races" widths={[320,500,700]}/><figcaption>Press and film crews were also part of the miniature race.</figcaption></figure></div><div><p>The Tour’s publicity caravan officially became part of the race in 1930. It travelled ahead of the peloton with decorated vehicles, advertising messages and small gifts for the roadside crowd. In miniature, the cyclists were therefore joined by service vans, camera cars and team cars carrying bicycles.</p><p>The little vehicles show another side of bicycle racing. A peloton is not made up of riders alone. There are mechanics, press, spare parts and brands wishing to be seen. A Molteni car, a Mavic service vehicle or a Campagnolo van gave the children’s race a caravan of its own.</p><p>Some miniatures were sold as ordinary toys, others as advertising or souvenir objects. For collectors today, the attraction lies precisely in that meeting of toy history, cycling history and commercial history.</p></div></section>
<section class="mini-gallery mini-gallery--cars"><figure><OptimizedImage src={molteniCar} alt="Orange Molteni Volvo miniature service car with bicycles on its roof" widths={[400,700,1000]}/><figcaption>Molteni service car with spare wheels and bicycles on the roof.</figcaption></figure><figure><OptimizedImage src={mavicCar} alt="Yellow Mavic miniature service car with a bicycle on its roof" widths={[400,700,1000]}/><figcaption>The Mavic car makes neutral race support part of the scene.</figcaption></figure><figure><OptimizedImage src={campagnoloVan} alt="Blue Campagnolo miniature van with a spare wheel on its roof" widths={[400,700,1000]}/><figcaption>Campagnolo van marked “assistenza tecnica corse”.</figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="mini-split"><div><p class="section-label">From toy to collection</p><h2>A peloton from several periods</h2><figure class="mini-side-figure"><OptimizedImage src={worldChampion} alt="Later miniature cyclist in a white world champion jersey" widths={[280,420,560]}/><figcaption>Later models could reproduce both teams and championship jerseys.</figcaption></figure></div><div><p>Many figures now stand in cabinets instead of on pavements. Older metal riders are collected by maker, mould, jersey, base and casting peculiarity. Later plastic and metal models reproduce teams and riders with far more sponsor marks and detail.</p><p>The layers of time should be kept separate. A worn, hand-painted post-war metal figure is not the same object as a later model in Bianchi, Molteni or world champion colours. Yet both belong to the same tradition: the desire to make the great road race small enough to hold in one hand.</p><p>That is why chipped paint does not necessarily make a poorer example. It may be evidence of what the figure was made to do. Before it became a collectible, it first had to reach the finish.</p></div></section>
<figure class="mini-wide"><OptimizedImage src={cameraCar} alt="Molteni miniature cyclist riding past a small camera car" widths={[480,768,1024]}/><figcaption>A later Molteni rider passes the camera car. Modern models continue the old game of recreating the whole race.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="period-sources"><p class="section-label">Sources and image note</p><div><p>The article is based on the collection’s fact-checked material. Manufacturing and casting details have been compared with the histories published by Fonderie Roger and Cofalu. The Tour’s official history documents the publicity caravan’s beginning in 1930. Every model shown was photographed in the collection; they come from different periods and should not all be understood as 1950s originals.</p><ul><li><a href="https://www.miniatures-pr.com/historique/">Miniatures P.R./Fonderie Roger: company history</a></li><li><a href="https://www.tourdefranceminiature.fr/les-cyclistes/2-les-cyclistes-en-plastique/cofalu/">Cofalu: family and product history</a></li><li><a href="https://www.letour.fr/en/news/2020/1930-the-tour-revolutionizes-3-10/1283535">Tour de France: the publicity caravan’s beginning in 1930</a></li><li><a href="https://www.cyclingboardgames.net/docs/poyer.pdf">Jean Poyer: the cyclist figure as a sports toy and homemade racing game</a></li></ul></div></section>
